{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Think step by step to translate the mathematical problem in natural language to Lean 4, and verify the consistency.\n证明：光线相继经过几个平行分界面的多层介质时，出射光线的方向只与入射方向及两边的折射率有关，与中间各层介质无关。如题图所示，请给出详细证明过程。"}, {"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "data:image/jpeg;base64,"}}]}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\n# Round 1 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"physsearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"Snell's law refraction refractive index parallel interfaces\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] SpeedOfLight.instCoeReal  (instance)\n    : Coe SpeedOfLight ℝ\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[2] SpeedOfLight.val  (definition)\n    SpeedOfLight → ℝ\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[3] SpeedOfLight.ctorIdx  (definition)\n    SpeedOfLight → ℕ\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[4] SpeedOfLight.recOn  (definition)\n    {motive : SpeedOfLight → Sort u} →\n  (t : SpeedOfLight) → ((val : ℝ) → (pos : 0 < val) → motive { val := val, pos := pos }) → motive t\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[5] SpeedOfLight.casesOn  (definition)\n    {motive : SpeedOfLight → Sort u} →\n  (t : SpeedOfLight) → ((val : ℝ) → (pos : 0 < val) → motive { val := val, pos := pos }) → motive t\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[6] SpeedOfLight.noConfusion  (definition)\n    {P : Sort u} → {t t' : SpeedOfLight} → t = t' → SpeedOfLight.noConfusionType P t t'\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[7] SpeedOfLight.mk.noConfusion  (definition)\n    {P : Sort u} →\n  {val : ℝ} →\n    {pos : 0 < val} →\n      {val' : ℝ} → {pos' : 0 < val'} → { val := val, pos := pos } = { val := val', pos := pos' } → (val = val' → P) → P\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[8] SpeedOfLight.val_one  (theorem)\n    : (1 : SpeedOfLight).val = 1\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"physsearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"Snell law refraction angle interface medium\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] SpeedOfLight.val  (definition)\n    SpeedOfLight → ℝ\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[2] SpeedOfLight.recOn  (definition)\n    {motive : SpeedOfLight → Sort u} →\n  (t : SpeedOfLight) → ((val : ℝ) → (pos : 0 < val) → motive { val := val, pos := pos }) → motive t\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[3] SpeedOfLight.ctorIdx  (definition)\n    SpeedOfLight → ℕ\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[4] SpeedOfLight.casesOn  (definition)\n    {motive : SpeedOfLight → Sort u} →\n  (t : SpeedOfLight) → ((val : ℝ) → (pos : 0 < val) → motive { val := val, pos := pos }) → motive t\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[5] SpeedOfLight.instCoeReal  (instance)\n    : Coe SpeedOfLight ℝ\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[6] SpeedOfLight.noConfusion  (definition)\n    {P : Sort u} → {t t' : SpeedOfLight} → t = t' → SpeedOfLight.noConfusionType P t t'\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[7] SpeedOfLight.instOne  (instance)\n    : One SpeedOfLight\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[8] SpeedOfLight.mk.noConfusion  (definition)\n    {P : Sort u} →\n  {val : ℝ} →\n    {pos : 0 < val} →\n      {val' : ℝ} → {pos' : 0 < val'} → { val := val, pos := pos } = { val := val', pos := pos' } → (val = val' → P) → P\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"physsearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"refraction optics light ray angle\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] SpeedOfLight.val  (definition)\n    SpeedOfLight → ℝ\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[2] SpeedOfLight.ctorIdx  (definition)\n    SpeedOfLight → ℕ\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[3] SpeedOfLight.casesOn  (definition)\n    {motive : SpeedOfLight → Sort u} →\n  (t : SpeedOfLight) → ((val : ℝ) → (pos : 0 < val) → motive { val := val, pos := pos }) → motive t\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[4] SpeedOfLight.recOn  (definition)\n    {motive : SpeedOfLight → Sort u} →\n  (t : SpeedOfLight) → ((val : ℝ) → (pos : 0 < val) → motive { val := val, pos := pos }) → motive t\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[5] SpeedOfLight.noConfusion  (definition)\n    {P : Sort u} → {t t' : SpeedOfLight} → t = t' → SpeedOfLight.noConfusionType P t t'\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[6] SpeedOfLight.instCoeReal  (instance)\n    : Coe SpeedOfLight ℝ\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[7] SpeedOfLight.val_pos  (theorem)\n    (c : SpeedOfLight) : 0 < (c : ℝ)\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[8] SpeedOfLight.pos  (theorem)\n    ∀ (self : SpeedOfLight), 0 < self.val\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"web_search\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"Snell's law formula n1 sin theta1 = n2 sin theta2 refraction\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n\n# Round 1 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nLean compiler reported errors: unexpected token '('; expected command\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Incorrect\n\n</round>\n\n# Round 2 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"physsearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"Snell's law refraction refractive index parallel interfaces\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] SpeedOfLight.instCoeReal  (instance)\n    : Coe SpeedOfLight ℝ\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[2] SpeedOfLight.val  (definition)\n    SpeedOfLight → ℝ\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[3] SpeedOfLight.ctorIdx  (definition)\n    SpeedOfLight → ℕ\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[4] SpeedOfLight.recOn  (definition)\n    {motive : SpeedOfLight → Sort u} →\n  (t : SpeedOfLight) → ((val : ℝ) → (pos : 0 < val) → motive { val := val, pos := pos }) → motive t\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[5] SpeedOfLight.casesOn  (definition)\n    {motive : SpeedOfLight → Sort u} →\n  (t : SpeedOfLight) → ((val : ℝ) → (pos : 0 < val) → motive { val := val, pos := pos }) → motive t\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[6] SpeedOfLight.noConfusion  (definition)\n    {P : Sort u} → {t t' : SpeedOfLight} → t = t' → SpeedOfLight.noConfusionType P t t'\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[7] SpeedOfLight.mk.noConfusion  (definition)\n    {P : Sort u} →\n  {val : ℝ} →\n    {pos : 0 < val} →\n      {val' : ℝ} → {pos' : 0 < val'} → { val := val, pos := pos } = { val := val', pos := pos' } → (val = val' → P) → P\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n[8] SpeedOfLight.val_one  (theorem)\n    : (1 : SpeedOfLight).val = 1\n    The speed of light is represented as an inductive type.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"web_search\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"Snell's law multiple parallel interfaces refractive index proof\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Snell's law\n    Snell's law is a formula used to describe the relationship between the angles of incidence and refraction, when referring to light or other waves passing ...\n    https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snell%27s_law\n[2] 8) Refraction at a plane surface | Snell's law at multiple parallel ...\n    8) Refraction at a plane surface | Snell's law at multiple parallel interfaces | refractive index ... #Physics #Proof of lens formula #for 10th & ...\n    https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JAfGnuJEPYw\n[3] Snell's Law -- The Law of Refraction\n    The constants n are the indices of refraction for the corresponding media. Tables of refractive indices for many substances have been compiled. n for Light of ...\n    https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~cass/courses/m309-01a/chu/Fundamentals/snell.htm\n[4] Snell's Law - Refraction\n    When dealing with refraction at a curved surface, we work only with the paraxial rays: Those that are both close to the optical axis and nearly parallel to it.\n    https://www.aao.org/Assets/76787c39-7c56-4312-8fb0-205aa6d2e0f5/637151349599070000/bo17-pdf?inline=1\n[5] 6.1.5: Snell's Law\n    If the index of refraction for the second material is greater than the first material, then the light will be refracted to a smaller angle.\n    https://eng.libretexts.org/Workbench/Materials_Science_for_Electrical_Engineering/06%3A_Thermal_Optical/6.01%3A_Optical_Properties/6.1.05%3A_Snell's_Law\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"web_search\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"Snell's law n1 sin theta1 = n2 sin theta2 multiple parallel layers\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Snell's law\n    Snell's law (also known as the Snell–Descartes law, is a formula used to describe the relationship between the angles of incidence and refraction,\n    https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snell%27s_law\n[2] Refraction and Snell's Law\n    Physics Ninja looks at Refraction and Snell's law and what happens to a light ray going from one medium to another.\n    https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RjukxPsaHsg\n[3] Snell's Law -- The Law of Refraction\n    We find that the incoming and outgoing light beams are actually parallel. n1sin(i) = n2sin(r) (n1/n2)sin(i) = sin(r) fraction is called total internal ...\n    https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~cass/courses/m309-01a/chu/Fundamentals/snell.htm\n[4] Snell's Law, Reflection, and Refraction\n    Snell's Law, which can be stated as nA Sinθ A = nB Sinθ B predicts how the ray will change direction as it passes from one medium into another.\n    https://www.optics4kids.org/what-is-optics/refraction/snell-s-law,-reflection,-and-refraction\n[5] Refraction Refractive Index N Snells Law N1 Sin 01 N2 Sin ...\n    Refraction: when light ray changes its direction in a new medium depending on the incident angle and the changes in speed. n1sinθ1 = n2sinθ2.\n    https://jackwestin.com/resources/mcat-content/geometrical-optics/refraction-refractive-index-n-snells-law-n1-sin-01-n2-sin-02\n</tool_response>\nNow I understand the problem. The statement is about Snell's law applied to multiple parallel interfaces. When light passes through several parallel layers of different media, the final outgoing angle depends only on the initial angle and the refractive indices of the first and last media, not on the intermediate layers.\n\nLet me formalize this in Lean 4:\n\n```lean4\nimport Physlib\n\n/--\nWhen light passes through multiple parallel interfaces between different media,\nthe direction of the outgoing ray depends only on the incident direction and\nthe refractive indices of the first and last media, independent of intermediate layers.\n\nThis is a consequence of Snell's law: n₁ * sin(θ₁) = n₂ * sin(θ₂)\n\nFor n parallel layers with refractive indices n₀, n₁, n₂, ..., nₖ,\napplying Snell's law at each interface gives:\nn₀ * sin(θ₀) = n₁ * sin(θ₁) = n₂ * sin(θ₂) = ... = nₖ * sin(θₖ)\n\nTherefore: n₀ * sin(θ₀) = nₖ * sin(θₖ)\n\nThe outgoing angle θₖ depends only on θ₀, n₀, and nₖ.\n-/\ntheorem snell_law_parallel_layers\n  (k : ℕ)\n  (n : Fin (k + 1) → ℝ)\n  (hn : ∀ i, 0 < n i)\n  (θ : Fin (k + 1) → ℝ)\n  (h_snell : ∀ i : Fin k, n i.castSucc * Real.sin (θ i.castSucc) = n i.succ * Real.sin (θ i.succ)) :\n  n 0 * Real.sin (θ 0) = n (Fin.last k) * Real.sin (θ (Fin.last k)) := by sorry\n```\n\n[syntax_rewrite] unicode subscript digits → ASCII\n```lean4\nimport Physlib\n\n/--\nWhen light passes through multiple parallel interfaces between different media,\nthe direction of the outgoing ray depends only on the incident direction and\nthe refractive indices of the first and last media, independent of intermediate layers.\n\nThis is a consequence of Snell's law: n1 * sin(θ1) = n2 * sin(θ2)\n\nFor n parallel layers with refractive indices n0, n1, n2, ..., nₖ,\napplying Snell's law at each interface gives:\nn0 * sin(θ0) = n1 * sin(θ1) = n2 * sin(θ2) = ... = nₖ * sin(θₖ)\n\nTherefore: n0 * sin(θ0) = nₖ * sin(θₖ)\n\nThe outgoing angle θₖ depends only on θ0, n0, and nₖ.\n-/\ntheorem snell_law_parallel_layers\n  (k : ℕ)\n  (n : Fin (k + 1) → ℝ)\n  (hn : ∀ i, 0 < n i)\n  (θ : Fin (k + 1) → ℝ)\n  (h_snell : ∀ i : Fin k, n i.castSucc * Real.sin (θ i.castSucc) = n i.succ * Real.sin (θ i.succ)) :\n  n 0 * Real.sin (θ 0) = n (Fin.last k) * Real.sin (θ (Fin.last k)) := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 2 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nYour brief analysis:\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: The problem asks to prove that for light passing through multiple parallel media layers, the final outgoing angle depends only on the initial angle and the refractive indices of the first and last layers, independent of intermediate layers. Key components: sequence of refractive indices $n_0, \\dots, n_k$, angles $\\theta_0, \\dots, \\theta_k$, Snell's Law at each interface ($n_i \\sin \\theta_i = n_{i+1} \\sin \\theta_{i+1}$), and the conclusion $n_0 \\sin \\theta_0 = n_k \\sin \\theta_k$.\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored): The theorem `snell_law_parallel_layers` takes `k` (number of interfaces), `n` (refractive indices for `k+1` media), `θ` (angles), and `h_snell` (Snell's law holding for each adjacent pair `i`, `i+1`). It concludes `n 0 * sin (θ 0) = n (Fin.last k) * sin (θ (Fin.last k))`, which corresponds to $n_0 \\sin \\theta_0 = n_k \\sin \\theta_k$.\n3. Comparative Verification: The Lean statement accurately models the physical setup (parallel layers via index chain), the physical law (Snell's law constraint `h_snell`), and the mathematical conclusion (invariant relating first and last layers). The use of `Fin` types correctly handles the indexing of layers and interfaces. The conclusion mathematically implies the dependency claim in the natural language statement.\n4. Conclusion: The Lean statement is a precise and exact formalization of the natural language problem.\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: All variables, constraints, and the logical conclusion match perfectly. The indexing (`Fin (k+1)` for media, `Fin k` for interfaces) is correct. The conclusion establishes the independence from intermediate layers by only involving indices 0 and `last k`.\n\nResult: Correct\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Correct\n\n</round>\n\n</think>\n\n<answer>\n\n```lean4\nimport Physlib\n\n/--\nWhen light passes through multiple parallel interfaces between different media,\nthe direction of the outgoing ray depends only on the incident direction and\nthe refractive indices of the first and last media, independent of intermediate layers.\n\nThis is a consequence of Snell's law: n1 * sin(θ1) = n2 * sin(θ2)\n\nFor n parallel layers with refractive indices n0, n1, n2, ..., nₖ,\napplying Snell's law at each interface gives:\nn0 * sin(θ0) = n1 * sin(θ1) = n2 * sin(θ2) = ... = nₖ * sin(θₖ)\n\nTherefore: n0 * sin(θ0) = nₖ * sin(θₖ)\n\nThe outgoing angle θₖ depends only on θ0, n0, and nₖ.\n-/\ntheorem snell_law_parallel_layers\n  (k : ℕ)\n  (n : Fin (k + 1) → ℝ)\n  (hn : ∀ i, 0 < n i)\n  (θ : Fin (k + 1) → ℝ)\n  (h_snell : ∀ i : Fin k, n i.castSucc * Real.sin (θ i.castSucc) = n i.succ * Real.sin (θ i.succ)) :\n  n 0 * Real.sin (θ 0) = n (Fin.last k) * Real.sin (θ (Fin.last k)) := by sorry\n```\n\n</answer>"}], "informal_statement": "证明：光线相继经过几个平行分界面的多层介质时，出射光线的方向只与入射方向及两边的折射率有关，与中间各层介质无关。如题图所示，请给出详细证明过程。", "num_rounds": 2, "num_tool_calls": 6, "tools_used": ["physsearch", "web_search"], "success": true, "syntax_pass": true, "syntax_rewrites": ["unicode subscript digits → ASCII"], "retrieval_domain": "physics", "dataset_name": "physunibench", "question_id": "1920", "category": "Optics", "has_image": true, "image_path": "/mnt/afs_reason/chendongyang/code/data/hf_datasets/PrismaX__PhysUniBench/images/1920.jpg"}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Think step by step to translate the mathematical problem in natural language to Lean 4, and verify the consistency.\n$N$ 个一阶正离子与 $N$ 个一阶负离子静止地在一条直线上等间距交错排列，相邻间距为 $a$，图中字母 $e$ 代表电子电量绝对值， 设 $N \\rightarrow \\infty$ ，试求其中一个负离子因受所有其余离子的电作用而具有的电势能 $W_{-}$\n\n[Image description: IMAGE 1:\nA linear array of alternating point charges, with magnitudes $+e$ and $-e$, is shown; the charges are separated by a constant distance $a$. The sequence continues indefinitely in both directions.]"}, {"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "data:image/jpeg;base64,"}}]}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\n# Round 1 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"infinite series sum alternating harmonic series\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Real.not_summable_natCast_inv  (theorem)\n    : ¬Summable (fun n => n⁻¹ : ℕ → ℝ)\n    The infinite series of reciprocals of natural numbers $\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n}$ is not summable in the real numbers, i.e., the harmonic series diverges.\n[2] Real.not_summable_one_div_natCast  (theorem)\n    : ¬Summable (fun n => 1 / n : ℕ → ℝ)\n    The infinite series $\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n}$ is not summable, i.e., it diverges.\n[3] Real.tendsto_sum_range_one_div_nat_succ_atTop  (theorem)\n    : Tendsto (fun n => ∑ i ∈ Finset.range n, (1 / (i + 1) : ℝ)) atTop atTop\n    The partial sums of the harmonic series $\\sum_{k=1}^n \\frac{1}{k}$ tend to infinity as $n$ approaches infinity.\n[4] Monotone.tendsto_alternating_series_of_tendsto_zero  (theorem)\n    (hfa : Monotone f) (hf0 : Tendsto f atTop (𝓝 0)) : ∃ l, Tendsto (fun n ↦ ∑ i ∈ range n, (-1) ^ i * f i) atTop (𝓝 l)\n    If $ f $ is a monotone function and $ \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} f(n) = 0 $, then the alternating series $ \\sum_{i=0}^{\\infty} (-1)^i f(i) $ converges to some limit $ l $.\n[5] Antitone.tendsto_alternating_series_of_tendsto_zero  (theorem)\n    (hfa : Antitone f) (hf0 : Tendsto f atTop (𝓝 0)) : ∃ l, Tendsto (fun n ↦ ∑ i ∈ range n, (-1) ^ i * f i) atTop (𝓝 l)\n    If $ f : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{R} $ is a non-increasing sequence satisfying $ \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} f(n) = 0 $, then the alternating series $ \\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} (-1)^i f(i) $ converges as $ n \\to \\infty $.\n[6] Summable.tendsto_alternating_series_tsum  (theorem)\n    {E} [Ring E] [UniformSpace E] [IsUniformAddGroup E] [CompleteSpace E] {f : ℕ → E} (hfs : Summable f) :\n  Tendsto (fun n => (∑ i ∈ range n, (-1) ^ i * f i)) atTop (𝓝 (∑' i : ℕ, (-1) ^ i * f i))\n    For a summable sequence $ f $ in a complete uniform ring $ E $ with a uniformly continuous additive group, the partial sums of the alternating series $ \\sum_{i=0}^n (-1)^i f(i) $ converge to the infinite sum $ \\sum_{i=0}^\\infty (-1)^i f(i) $ as $ n $ approaches infinity.\n[7] Monotone.alternating_series_le_tendsto  (theorem)\n    (hfl : Tendsto (fun n ↦ ∑ i ∈ range n, (-1) ^ i * f i) atTop (𝓝 l)) (hfm : Monotone f) (k : ℕ) :\n  ∑ i ∈ range (2 * k + 1), (-1) ^ i * f i ≤ l\n    For a non-increasing sequence $ f $ where the alternating series $ \\sum_{i=0}^\\infty (-1)^i f(i) $ converges to $ l $, the partial sum up to $ 2k+1 $ terms satisfies $ \\sum_{i=0}^{2k} (-1)^i f(i) \\leq l $ for all $ k \\in \\mathbb{N} $.\n[8] harmonic  (definition)\n    : ℕ → ℚ\n    The function maps a natural number $ n $ to the $ n $-th harmonic number $ H_n $, defined as the sum $ \\sum_{k=1}^n \\frac{1}{k} $, where each term is considered as a rational number.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"alternating harmonic series sum log 2\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Real.hasSum_log_sub_log_of_abs_lt_one  (theorem)\n    {x : ℝ} (h : |x| < 1) : HasSum (fun k : ℕ => (2 : ℝ) * (1 / (2 * k + 1)) * x ^ (2 * k + 1)) (log (1 + x) - log (1 - x))\n    For any real number $x$ with $|x| < 1$, the infinite series $\\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\frac{2}{2k+1} x^{2k+1}$ converges to $\\log(1+x) - \\log(1-x)$.\n[2] Complex.hasSum_taylorSeries_log  (theorem)\n    {z : ℂ} (hz : ‖z‖ < 1) : HasSum (fun n : ℕ ↦ (-1) ^ (n + 1) * z ^ n / n) (log (1 + z))\n    For any complex number $ z $ with $ |z| < 1 $, the infinite series $ \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{(-1)^{n+1} z^n}{n} $ converges to the complex logarithm $ \\log(1 + z) $.\n[3] Real.hasSum_pow_div_log_of_abs_lt_one  (theorem)\n    {x : ℝ} (h : |x| < 1) : HasSum (fun n : ℕ => x ^ (n + 1) / (n + 1)) (-log (1 - x))\n    For any real number $ x $ with $ |x| < 1 $, the infinite series $ \\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1} $ converges to $ -\\ln(1 - x) $.\n[4] Real.hasSum_log_one_add_inv  (theorem)\n    {a : ℝ} (h : 0 < a) :\n  HasSum (fun k : ℕ => (2 : ℝ) * (1 / (2 * k + 1)) * (1 / (2 * a + 1)) ^ (2 * k + 1)) (log (1 + a⁻¹))\n    For any positive real number $a > 0$, the infinite series $\\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\frac{2}{2k+1} \\left(\\frac{1}{2a+1}\\right)^{2k+1}$ converges to $\\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{a}\\right)$.\n[5] Real.hasSum_log_one_add  (theorem)\n    {a : ℝ} (h : 0 ≤ a) : HasSum (fun k : ℕ => (2 : ℝ) * (1 / (2 * k + 1)) * (a / (a + 2)) ^ (2 * k + 1)) (log (1 + a))\n    For any real number $a \\geq 0$, the infinite series $\\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\frac{2}{2k + 1} \\left(\\frac{a}{a + 2}\\right)^{2k + 1}$ converges to $\\ln(1 + a)$.\n[6] Complex.hasSum_taylorSeries_neg_log  (theorem)\n    {z : ℂ} (hz : ‖z‖ < 1) : HasSum (fun n : ℕ ↦ z ^ n / n) (-log (1 - z))\n    For any complex number $ z $ with $ |z| < 1 $, the infinite series $ \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{z^n}{n} $ converges to $ -\\log(1 - z) $, where $ \\log $ denotes the complex logarithm.\n[7] Real.tendsto_harmonic_sub_log  (theorem)\n    : Tendsto (fun n : ℕ ↦ harmonic n - log n) atTop (𝓝 eulerMascheroniConstant)\n    The limit as $ n $ approaches infinity of the difference between the $ n $-th harmonic number $ H_n $ and the natural logarithm of $ n $ is equal to the Euler-Masc