e to a limit $l$, then for any natural number $k$, the limit satisfies $l \\leq \\sum_{i=0}^{2k} (-1)^i f(i)$.\n[5] Complex.hasSum_taylorSeries_neg_log  (theorem)\n    {z : ℂ} (hz : ‖z‖ < 1) : HasSum (fun n : ℕ ↦ z ^ n / n) (-log (1 - z))\n    For any complex number $ z $ with $ |z| < 1 $, the infinite series $ \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{z^n}{n} $ converges to $ -\\log(1 - z) $, where $ \\log $ denotes the complex logarithm.\n[6] alternating_series_error_bound  (theorem)\n    {E} [Ring E] [LinearOrder E] [IsOrderedRing E] [UniformSpace E] [IsUniformAddGroup E] [CompleteSpace E]\n  [OrderClosedTopology E] (f : ℕ → E) (hfa : Antitone f) (hfs : Summable f) (n : ℕ) :\n  |(∑' i : ℕ, (-1) ^ i * f i) - (∑ i ∈ range n, (-1) ^ i * f i)| ≤ f n\n    For an antitone and summable sequence $ f : \\mathbb{N} \\to E $ in an ordered ring $ E $ equipped with a complete uniform structure and order-closed topology, the absolute difference between the infinite alternating sum $ \\sum_{i=0}^\\infty (-1)^i f(i) $ and its $ n $-th partial sum $ \\sum_{i=0}^n (-1)^i f(i) $ satisfies $ \\left| \\sum_{i=0}^\\infty (-1)^i f(i) - \\sum_{i=0}^n (-1)^i f(i) \\right| \\leq f(n) $.\n[7] Antitone.tendsto_alternating_series_of_tendsto_zero  (theorem)\n    (hfa : Antitone f) (hf0 : Tendsto f atTop (𝓝 0)) : ∃ l, Tendsto (fun n ↦ ∑ i ∈ range n, (-1) ^ i * f i) atTop (𝓝 l)\n    If $ f : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{R} $ is a non-increasing sequence satisfying $ \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} f(n) = 0 $, then the alternating series $ \\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} (-1)^i f(i) $ converges as $ n \\to \\infty $.\n[8] Real.tendsto_sum_pi_div_four  (theorem)\n    : Tendsto (fun k => ∑ i ∈ range k, (-1 : ℝ) ^ i / (2 * i + 1)) atTop (𝓝 (π / 4))\n    The alternating series $\\sum_{i=0}^\\infty \\frac{(-1)^i}{2i+1}$ converges to $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$, i.e., $\\lim_{k \\to \\infty} \\sum_{i=0}^{k-1} \\frac{(-1)^i}{2i+1} = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"tsum (-1)^n / n natural logarithm 2\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n\n# Round 1 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nLean compiler reported errors: unexpected token '('; expected command\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Incorrect\n\n</round>\n\n# Round 2 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"physsearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"electrostatic potential energy point charges Coulomb\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Electromagnetism.EMSystem.coulombConstant  (definition)\n    : ℝ\n    Coulomb's constant is defined as \\( k = \\frac{1}{4 \\pi \\varepsilon_0} \\), where \\( \\varepsilon_0 \\) is the electric permittivity of an electromagnetic system.\n[2] Electromagnetism.DistElectromagneticPotential.threeDimPointParticle_scalarPotential  (theorem)\n    (𝓕 : FreeSpace) (q : ℝ) (r₀ : Space 3) :\n  (threeDimPointParticle 𝓕 q r₀).scalarPotential 𝓕.c =\n    Space.constantTime\n      (distOfFunction (fun x => (q / (4 * π * 𝓕.ε₀)) • ‖x - r₀‖⁻¹) (((IsDistBounded.inv_shift _).const_mul_fun _)))\n    The scalar potential of a stationary point particle located at \\( r_0 \\) in three-dimensional space is defined as the distribution given by \\( (\\mathcal{F} : \\text{FreeSpace}) (q : \\mathbb{R}) (r_0 : \\text{Space} \\ 3) : (\\text{threeDimPointParticle} \\mathcal{F} \\ q \\ r_0).\\text{scalarPotential} \\mathcal{F}.\\varepsilon_0 = \\text{Space.constantTime} (\\text{distOfFunction} (\\text{fun} \\ x => (q / (4 \\pi \\mathcal{F}.\\varepsilon_0)) \\cdot \\|x - r_0\\|^{-1}) (((\\text{IsDistBounded.inv_shift} \\_).\\text{const_mul_fun} \\_))) \\) in the context of electromagnetism.\n[3] QuantumMechanics.HydrogenAtom.coulomb_potential  (theorem)\n    ∀ (self : QuantumMechanics.HydrogenAtom), self.potential = fun x => -self.k * ‖x‖⁻¹\n    For any hydrogen atom represented as a quantum system, the Coulomb potential is defined as \\( V(x) = -k \\cdot \\|x\\|^{-1} \\), where \\( k \\) is a constant associated with the hydrogen atom and \\( x \\) represents the position vector.\n[4] Electromagnetism.DistElectromagneticPotential.threeDimPointParticle  (definition)\n    (𝓕 : FreeSpace) (q : ℝ) (r₀ : Space 3) : DistElectromagneticPotential 3\n    The electromagnetic potential of a point particle stationary at \\( r_0 \\) in three-dimensional space is defined.\n[5] Electromagnetism.DistElectromagneticPotential.oneDimPointParticle_scalarPotential  (theorem)\n    (𝓕 : FreeSpace) (q : ℝ) (r₀ : Space 1) :\n  (oneDimPointParticle 𝓕 q r₀).scalarPotential 𝓕.c =\n    Space.constantTime (distOfFunction (fun x => -((q * 𝓕.μ₀ * 𝓕.c ^ 2) / (2)) • ‖x - r₀‖) (by fun_prop))\n    For a distributional electromagnetic potential in the context of electromagnetism, the scalar potential of a point particle stationary at position \\( r_0 \\) in one-dimensional space is defined.\n[6] Electromagnetism.DistElectromagneticPotential.threeDimPointParticle_eq_distTranslate  (theorem)\n    (𝓕 : FreeSpace) (q : ℝ) (r₀ : Space 3) :\n  threeDimPointParticle 𝓕 q r₀ =\n    ((SpaceTime.distTimeSlice 𝓕.c).symm <|\n      constantTime <|\n        distTranslate (basis.repr r₀) <|\n          distOfFunction (fun x => (((q * 𝓕.μ₀ * 𝓕.c) / (4 * π)) * ‖x‖⁻¹) • Lorentz.Vector.basis (Sum.inl 0))\n            ((IsDistBounded.inv.const_mul_fun _).smul_const _))\n    The electromagnetic potential of a point particle stationary at \\( r_0 \\) in three-dimensional space is defined.\n[7] Electromagnetism.DistElectromagneticPotential.threeDimPointParticle_electricField  (theorem)\n    (𝓕 : FreeSpace) (q : ℝ) (r₀ : Space 3) :\n  (threeDimPointParticle 𝓕 q r₀).electricField 𝓕.c =\n    (q / (4 * π * 𝓕.ε₀)) •\n      constantTime\n        (distOfFunction (fun x : Space 3 => ‖x - r₀‖ ^ (-3 : ℤ) • basis.repr (x - r₀))\n          ((IsDistBounded.zpow_smul_repr_self (-3 : ℤ) (by omega)).comp_sub_right r₀))\n    For any free space \\( \\mathcal{F} \\), charge \\( q \\), and position \\( r_0 \\) in three-dimensional space, the electric field generated by a stationary point particle is given by the formula \\( (q / (4 \\pi \\mathcal{F}.\\varepsilon_0)) \\cdot ext{constantTime} \\left( ext{distOfFunction} \\left( \\lambda x : ext{Space} \\, 3, \\, \\|x - r_0\\|^{-3} \\cdot ext{basis.repr}(x - r_0) ight) ight) \\). This expression captures the spatial and temporal variations of the electric field due to the point charge.\n[8] Electromagnetism.DistElectromagneticPotential.threeDimPointParticle_vectorPotential  (theorem)\n    (𝓕 : FreeSpace) (q : ℝ) (r₀ : Space 3) : (threeDimPointParticle 𝓕 q r₀).vectorPotential 𝓕.c = 0\n    For any free space \\( \\mathcal{F} \\), charge \\( q \\), and position \\( r_0 \\) of a stationary point particle in three-dimensional space, the vector potential of the electromagnetic potential at that point is defined as zero, i.e., \\( (threeDimPointParticle \\mathcal{F} \\ q \\ r_0).vectorPotential \\mathcal{F}.c = 0 \\).\n</tool_response>\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Basic\nimport Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic\nimport Mathlib.Topology.Algebra.InfiniteSum.Basic\n\nopen Real\n\n/--\nThe electrostatic potential energy of a negative ion in an infinite 1D chain of \nalternating charges +e and -e with spacing a. \nThe Coulomb constant is denoted by K (where K = 1/(4*pi*epsilon_0)).\nThe result is derived from the limit as N -> infinity, corresponding to the \nalternating harmonic series which sums to -ln(2).\n-/\ntheorem potential_energy_1d_ionic_chain (e a K : ℝ) (he : e > 0) (ha : a > 0) (hK : K > 0) :\n  let term : ℕ → ℝ := fun n => if n = 0 then 0 else (2 * K * e^2 / a) * ((-1 : ℝ)^n / (n : ℝ))\n  HasSum term (-(2 * K * e^2 * log 2) / a) := by sorry\n```<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"alternating harmonic series sum log 2\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Real.hasSum_log_sub_log_of_abs_lt_one  (theorem)\n    {x : ℝ} (h : |x| < 1) : HasSum (fun k : ℕ => (2 : ℝ) * (1 / (2 * k + 1)) * x ^ (2 * k + 1)) (log (1 + x) - log (1 - x))\n    For any real number $x$ with $|x| < 1$, the infinite series $\\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\frac{2}{2k+1} x^{2k+1}$ converges to $\\log(1+x) - \\log(1-x)$.\n[2] Complex.hasSum_taylorSeries_log  (theorem)\n    {z : ℂ} (hz : ‖z‖ < 1) : HasSum (fun n : ℕ ↦ (-1) ^ (n + 1) * z ^ n / n) (log (1 + z))\n    For any complex number $ z $ with $ |z| < 1 $, the infinite series $ \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{(-1)^{n+1} z^n}{n} $ converges to the complex logarithm $ \\log(1 + z) $.\n[3] Real.hasSum_pow_div_log_of_abs_lt_one  (theorem)\n    {x : ℝ} (h : |x| < 1) : HasSum (fun n : ℕ => x ^ (n + 1) / (n + 1)) (-log (1 - x))\n    For any real number $ x $ with $ |x| < 1 $, the infinite series $ \\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1} $ converges to $ -\\ln(1 - x) $.\n[4] Real.hasSum_log_one_add_inv  (theorem)\n    {a : ℝ} (h : 0 < a) :\n  HasSum (fun k : ℕ => (2 : ℝ) * (1 / (2 * k + 1)) * (1 / (2 * a + 1)) ^ (2 * k + 1)) (log (1 + a⁻¹))\n    For any positive real number $a > 0$, the infinite series $\\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\frac{2}{2k+1} \\left(\\frac{1}{2a+1}\\right)^{2k+1}$ converges to $\\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{a}\\right)$.\n[5] Real.hasSum_log_one_add  (theorem)\n    {a : ℝ} (h : 0 ≤ a) : HasSum (fun k : ℕ => (2 : ℝ) * (1 / (2 * k + 1)) * (a / (a + 2)) ^ (2 * k + 1)) (log (1 + a))\n    For any real number $a \\geq 0$, the infinite series $\\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\frac{2}{2k + 1} \\left(\\frac{a}{a + 2}\\right)^{2k + 1}$ converges to $\\ln(1 + a)$.\n[6] Complex.hasSum_taylorSeries_neg_log  (theorem)\n    {z : ℂ} (hz : ‖z‖ < 1) : HasSum (fun n : ℕ ↦ z ^ n / n) (-log (1 - z))\n    For any complex number $ z $ with $ |z| < 1 $, the infinite series $ \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{z^n}{n} $ converges to $ -\\log(1 - z) $, where $ \\log $ denotes the complex logarithm.\n[7] Real.tendsto_harmonic_sub_log  (theorem)\n    : Tendsto (fun n : ℕ ↦ harmonic n - log n) atTop (𝓝 eulerMascheroniConstant)\n    The limit as $ n $ approaches infinity of the difference between the $ n $-th harmonic number $ H_n $ and the natural logarithm of $ n $ is equal to the Euler-Mascheroni constant $ \\gamma $.\n[8] padicValRat_two_harmonic  (theorem)\n    (n : ℕ) : padicValRat 2 (harmonic n) = -Nat.log 2 n\n    For any natural number $ n $, the 2-adic valuation of the $ n $-th harmonic number $ H_n $ is equal to $ -\\lfloor \\log_2 n \\rfloor $.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"Real.tsum alternating series negative log\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Real.hasSum_pow_div_log_of_abs_lt_one  (theorem)\n    {x : ℝ} (h : |x| < 1) : HasSum (fun n : ℕ => x ^ (n + 1) / (n + 1)) (-log (1 - x))\n    For any real number $ x $ with $ |x| < 1 $, the infinite series $ \\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1} $ converges to $ -\\ln(1 - x) $.\n[2] Real.hasSum_log_sub_log_of_abs_lt_one  (theorem)\n    {x : ℝ} (h : |x| < 1) : HasSum (fun k : ℕ => (2 : ℝ) * (1 / (2 * k + 1)) * x ^ (2 * k + 1)) (log (1 + x) - log (1 - x))\n    For any real number $x$ with $|x| < 1$, the infinite series $\\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\frac{2}{2k+1} x^{2k+1}$ converges to $\\log(1+x) - \\log(1-x)$.\n[3] Real.sum_range_le_log_div  (theorem)\n    {x : ℝ} (h₀ : 0 ≤ x) (h : x < 1) (n : ℕ) :\n  ∑ i ∈ range n, x ^ (2 * i + 1) / (2 * i + 1) ≤ 1 / 2 * log ((1 + x) / (1 - x))\n    For any real number $ x $ satisfying $ 0 \\leq x < 1 $ and any natural number $ n $, the sum $ \\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} \\frac{x^{2i+1}}{2i+1} $ is less than or equal to $ \\frac{1}{2} \\ln\\left( \\frac{1 + x}{1 - x} \\right) $.\n[4] Real.sum_range_sub_log_div_le  (theorem)\n    {x : ℝ} (h : |x| < 1) (n : ℕ) :\n  |1 / 2 * log ((1 + x) / (1 - x)) - ∑ i ∈ range n, x ^ (2 * i + 1) / (2 * i + 1)| ≤ |x| ^ (2 * n + 1) / (1 - x ^ 2)\n    For any real number $x$ with $|x| < 1$ and natural number $n$, the absolute difference between $\\frac{1}{2} \\ln\\left(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}\\right)$ and the partial sum $\\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} \\frac{x^{2i+1}}{2i+1}$ is bounded above by $\\frac{|x|^{2n+1}}{1 - x^2}$.\n[5] Real.tendsto_sum_pi_div_four  (theorem)\n    : Tendsto (fun k => ∑ i ∈ range k, (-1 : ℝ) ^ i / (2 * i + 1)) atTop (𝓝 (π / 4))\n    The alternating series $\\sum_{i=0}^\\infty \\frac{(-1)^i}{2i+1}$ converges to $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$, i.e., $\\lim_{k \\to \\infty} \\sum_{i=0}^{k-1} \\frac{(-1)^i}{2i+1} = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"sum (-1)^n / n = -log 2\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n\n# Round 2 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nYour brief analysis:\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: The problem asks for the electrostatic potential energy $W_{-}$ of a single negative ion in an infinite 1D chain of alternating charges $\\pm e$ with spacing $a$. The energy is the sum of interactions with all other ions. For a target ion $-e$ at origin, ions at distance $n \\cdot a$ have charge $(-1)^n (-e)$ (assuming alternating pattern starts with $+e$ at $n=1$). The interaction energy for the pair at distance $n \\cdot a$ (left and right) is $2 \\cdot \\frac{K(-e)((-1)^n(-e))}{n a} = \\frac{2 K e^2}{a} \\frac{(-1)^n}{n}$. The total energy is the sum over $n=1$ to $\\infty$, which converges to $-\\frac{2 K e^2 \\ln 2}{a}$.\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored): The theorem defines `term n` as the contribution from the $n$-th shell of neighbors. `term 0 = 0`. For $n \\ge 1$, `term n = (2 * K * e^2 / a) * ((-1)^n / n)`. It asserts `HasSum term` equals `-(2 * K * e^2 * log 2) / a`. Parameters $e, a, K$ are positive reals.\n3. Comparative Verification: \n   - The `term` definition matches the physical derivation (factor 2 for symmetry, $(-1)^n$ for alternating attraction/repulsion, $1/n$ for distance).\n   - The sum value matches the analytical result of the alternating harmonic series scaled by physical constants.\n   - `HasSum` correctly formalizes the $N \\to \\infty$ limit.\n   - Types and constraints ($e, a, K > 0$) are consistent.\n   - Syntax is valid Lean 4 (imports, `let` in type, `HasSum`).\n4. Conclusion: The Lean statement is a mathematically exact and syntactically valid formalization of the physics problem.\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: All components (variables, series definition, limit value, constraints) correspond exactly between the natural language problem and the Lean theorem. The previous syntax error mentioned in the prompt context does not apply to the provided code block, which is valid.\n\nResult: Correct\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Correct\n\n</round>\n\n</think>\n\n<answer>\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Basic\nimport Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic\nimport Mathlib.Topology.Algebra.InfiniteSum.Basic\n\nopen Real\n\n/--\nThe electrostatic potential energy of a negative ion in an infinite 1D chain of \nalternating charges +e and -e with spacing a. \nThe Coulomb constant is denoted by K (where K = 1/(4*pi*epsilon_0)).\nThe result is derived from the limit as N -> infinity, corresponding to the \nalternating harmonic series which sums to -ln(2).\n-/\ntheorem potential_energy_1d_ionic_chain (e a K : ℝ) (he : e > 0) (ha : a > 0) (hK : K > 0) :\n  let term : ℕ → ℝ := fun n => if n = 0 then 0 else (2 * K * e^2 / a) * ((-1 : ℝ)^n / (n : ℝ))\n  HasSum term (-(2 * K * e^2 * log 2) / a) := by sorry\n```\n\n</answer>"}], "informal_statement": "$N$ 个一阶正离子与 $N$ 个一阶负离子静止地在一条直线上等间距交错排列，相邻间距为 $a$，图中字母 $e$ 代表电子电量绝对值， 设 $N \\rightarrow \\infty$ ，试求其中一个负离子因受所有其余离子的电作用而具有的电势能 $W_{-}$\n\n[Image description: IMAGE 1:\nA linear array of alternating point charges, with magnitudes $+e$ and $-e$, is shown; the charges are separated by a constant distance $a$. The sequence continues indefinitely in both directions.]", "num_rounds": 2, "num_tool_calls": 6, "tools_used": ["leansearch", "physsearch"], "success": true, "syntax_pass": true, "syntax_rewrites": [], "retrieval_domain": "math", "dataset_name": "seephys", "question_id": "648", "category": "EM", "has_image": true, "image_path": "/mnt/afs_reason/chendongyang/code/llm4math/ReForm/data/train/sft_collect/seeds/physics/seephys_images/648.png"}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Think step by step to translate the mathematical problem in natural language to Lean 4, and verify the consistency.\n考虑一单色光入射到一狭缝上。见题图2.71(1)<image>。假定入射光波长为 $\\lambda$，并且缝宽 $w = 5\\lambda$。计算强度对角度的关系；给出第一极小的位置并估计第一极大位置。"}, {"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "data:image/jpeg;base64,"}}]}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\n# Round 1 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"physsearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"single slit diffraction intensity pattern wavelength\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] QuantumMechanics.OneDimension.HilbertSpace.planewaveFunctional  (definition)\n    (k : ℝ) : 𝓢(ℝ, ℂ) →L[ℂ] ℂ\n    The function maps a real number \\( k \\) to the plane wave represented as the tempered distribution \\( \\exp(2\\pi i k x) \\), which is a member of the dual of the Schwartz submodule of the Hilbert space.\n[2] ClassicalMechanics.transverseHarmonicPlaneWave  (definition)\n    (k : WaveVector) (f₀x f₀y ω δx δy : ℝ) (hk : k = EuclideanSpace.single 2 (ω / c)) :\n  Time → Space → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 3)\n    This definition describes a transverse monochromatic time-harmonic plane wave, where the direction of propagation is given by \\( \\text{EuclideanSpace.single} \\ 2 \\ 1 \\). The amplitudes are represented by \\( f₀x \\) and \\( f₀y \\), while \\( \\omega \\) denotes the angular frequency. The phases for the respective components \\( fx \\) and \\( fy \\) are given by \\( \\delta x \\) and \\( \\delta y \\).\n[3] Electromagnetism.ElectromagneticPotential.harmonicWaveX  (definition)\n    (𝓕 : FreeSpace) (k : ℝ) (E₀ : Fin d → ℝ) (φ : Fin d → ℝ) : ElectromagneticPotential d.succ\n    The electromagnetic potential for a harmonic wave traveling in the \\( x \\)-direction is defined with wave number \\( k \\).\n[4] Electromagnetism.ElectromagneticPotential.IsPlaneWave.electricFunction  (definition)\n    {d : ℕ} {𝓕 : FreeSpace} {A : ElectromagneticPotential d} {s : Direction d} (hA : IsPlaneWave 𝓕 A s) :\n  ℝ → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)\n    The function maps a real number \\( t \\) to the corresponding electric field of a plane wave in the Euclidean space \\( \\mathbb{R}^d \\).\n[5] ClassicalMechanics.harmonicWave  (definition)\n    (a g : ℝ → Space d → ℝ) (ω : WaveVector d → ℝ) (k : WaveVector d) : Time → Space d → ℝ\n    The function describes a time-harmonic wave in terms of angular frequency \\( \\omega \\) and wave vector \\( k \\). Specifically, it takes a time \\( t \\) and a position in space \\( r \\) to return the wave's amplitude, which is given by the product of a function evaluated at \\( \\omega k \\) and the cosine of the expression \\( \\omega k \\cdot t - g(\\omega k) r \\).\n[6] Electromagnetism.ElectromagneticPotential.harmonicWaveX_vectorPotential_succ  (theorem)\n    {d} (𝓕 : FreeSpace) (k : ℝ) (E₀ : Fin d → ℝ) (φ : Fin d → ℝ) (t : Time) (x : Space d.succ) (i : Fin d) :\n  (harmonicWaveX 𝓕 k E₀ φ).vectorPotential 𝓕.c t x i.succ =\n    -E₀ i * 1 / (𝓕.c * k) * Real.sin (k * (t.val * 𝓕.c - x 0) + φ i)\n    The electromagnetic potential for a harmonic wave traveling in the \\( x \\)-direction is defined with wave number \\( k \\).\n[7] QuantumMechanics.OneDimension.HilbertSpace.planewaveFunctional_apply  (theorem)\n    (k : ℝ) (ψ : 𝓢(ℝ, ℂ)) : planewaveFunctional k ψ = 𝓕 ψ k\n    The function maps a real number \\( k \\) to the plane wave represented as the tempered distribution \\( \\exp(2\\pi i k x) \\), which is a member of the dual of the Schwartz submodule of the Hilbert space.\n[8] ClassicalMechanics.WaveEquation  (definition)\n    {d} (f : Time → Space d → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)) (t : Time) (x : Space d) (c : ℝ) : Prop\n    The general form of the wave equation describes how a function \\( f \\) propagates through space and time, where \\( c \\) represents the propagation speed.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"physsearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"Fraunhofer diffraction single slit intensity sinc function\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Cosmology.SpatialGeometry.tendsto_sin_rx_over_x  (theorem)\n    (r : ℝ) : Tendsto (fun x : ℝ => Real.sin (r * x) / x) (𝓝[≠] 0) (𝓝 r)\n    For any real number \\( r \\), the limit of the function \\( \\frac{\\sin(r \\cdot x)}{x} \\) as \\( x \\) approaches zero (excluding zero) is equal to \\( r \\). This result establishes the behavior of the sine function scaled by \\( r \\) near the origin.\n[2] Physlib.Distribution.fourierTransform_apply  (theorem)\n    (u : E→d[ℂ] F) (η : 𝓢(E, ℂ)) : u.fourierTransform E F η = u (fourierTransformCLM ℂ η)\n    The Fourier transform of a distribution \\( u \\) is denoted as \\( F\\{u\\} \\) and is defined such that for a test function \\( \\eta \\), the Fourier transform satisfies \\( F\\{u\\}(\\eta) := u(F\\{\\eta\\}) \\).\n[3] Physlib.Distribution.fourierTransform  (definition)\n    : (E→d[ℂ] F) →ₗ[ℂ] (E→d[ℂ] F)\n    The Fourier transform of a distribution \\( u \\) is denoted as \\( F\\{u\\} \\) and is defined such that for a test function \\( \\eta \\), the Fourier transform satisfies \\( F\\{u\\}(\\eta) := u(F\\{\\eta\\}) \\).\n[4] Cosmology.SpatialGeometry.limit_S_sphere  (theorem)\n    (r : ℝ) : Tendsto (fun k : ℝ => k * Real.sin (r / k)) atTop (𝓝 r)\n    For any positive real number $r$, the limit of the function $k \\mapsto k \\cdot \\sin\\left(\\frac{r}{k}\\right)$ as $k$ approaches infinity is equal to $r$. This result demonstrates the behavior of the sine function when scaled by a variable that approaches infinity.\n[5] QuantumMechanics.OneDimension.HilbertSpace.planewaveFunctional  (definition)\n    (k : ℝ) : 𝓢(ℝ, ℂ) →L[ℂ] ℂ\n    The function maps a real number \\( k \\) to the plane wave represented as the tempered distribution \\( \\exp(2\\pi i k x) \\), which is a member of the dual of the Schwartz submodule of the Hilbert space.\n[6] Electromagnetism.ElectromagneticPotential.harmonicWaveX  (definition)\n    (𝓕 : FreeSpace) (k : ℝ) (E₀ : Fin d → ℝ) (φ : Fin d → ℝ) : ElectromagneticPotential d.succ\n    The electromagnetic potential for a harmonic wave traveling in the \\( x \\)-direction is defined with wave number \\( k \\).\n[7] QuantumMechanics.OneDimension.HilbertSpace.planewaveFunctional_apply  (theorem)\n    (k : ℝ) (ψ : 𝓢(ℝ, ℂ)) : planewaveFunctional k ψ = 𝓕 ψ k\n    The function maps a real number \\( k \\) to the plane wave represented as the tempered distribution \\( \\exp(2\\pi i k x) \\), which is a member of the dual of the Schwartz submodule of the Hilbert space.\n[8] ClassicalMechanics.planeWave_differentiable  (theorem)\n    {s : Direction d} {f₀ : ℝ → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)} (h' : Differentiable ℝ f₀) : Differentiable ℝ ↿(planeWave f₀ c s)\n    For a direction \\( s \\) in space and a differentiable function \\( f_0 : \\mathbb{R} o ext{EuclideanSpace} \\mathbb{R} ( ext{Fin } d ) \\), the plane wave function defined by \\( ext{planeWave}(f_0, c, s) \\) is also differentiable.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"physsearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"diffraction optics slit angular intensity minimum maximum\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Electromagnetism.ElectromagneticPotential.harmonicWaveX_isExtrema  (theorem)\n    {d} (𝓕 : FreeSpace) (k : ℝ) (hk : k ≠ 0) (E₀ : Fin d → ℝ) (φ : Fin d → ℝ) : IsExtrema 𝓕 (harmonicWaveX 𝓕 k E₀ φ) 0\n    For any free space \\( \\mathcal{F} \\), wave number \\( k \\) (where \\( k \\neq 0 \\)), and functions \\( E_0 \\) and \\( \\phi \\) defined on a finite dimensional space, the electromagnetic potential defined by the harmonic wave in the direction of \\( x \\) is an extremum of the Lagrangian if the variational gradient of the Lagrangian function evaluated at \\( \\mathcal{F} \\) and the Lorentz current density equals zero, expressed as \\( \\text{gradLagrangian} \\ \\mathcal{F} \\ \\text{harmonicWaveX} \\ \\mathcal{F} \\ k \\ E_0 \\ \\phi = 0 \\).\n[2] Space.lintegral_radialMeasure  (theorem)\n    {d : ℕ} (f : Space d → ENNReal) (hf : Measurable f) :\n  ∫⁻ x, f x ∂radialAngularMeasure = ∫⁻ x, ENNReal.ofReal (1 / ‖x‖ ^ (d - 1)) * f x\n    For any measurable function $f$ defined on a space $\\text{Space} \\ d$, the integral of $f$ with respect to the radial angular measure is given by the equation $\\int⁻ x, f(x) \\partial \\text{radialAngularMeasure} = \\int⁻ x, \\text{ENNReal.ofReal}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\|x\\|^{d - 1}}\\right) \\cdot f(x)$.\n[3] Space.instSFiniteRadialAngularMeasure  (instance)\n    (d : ℕ) : SFinite (radialAngularMeasure (d := d))\n    The measure on a space \\( \\text{Space} \\ d \\) is weighted by \\( \\frac{1}{\\|x\\|^{d - 1}} \\), where \\( d \\) is a natural number that can be parameterized with a default value of 3.\n[4] Electromagnetism.DistElectromagneticPotential.isExtrema_iff_gradLagrangian  (theorem)\n    {𝓕 : FreeSpace} (A : DistElectromagneticPotential d) (J : DistLorentzCurrentDensity d) :\n  IsExtrema 𝓕 A J ↔ A.gradLagrangian 𝓕 J = 0\n    For a distributional electromagnetic potential \\( A \\), the condition that \\( A \\) is an extremum of the Lagrangian is equivalent to the variational gradient of the Lagrangian \\( A.gradLagrangian \\) being equal to zero when evaluated with respect to a free space \\( 𝓕 \\) and a distributional Lorentz current density \\( J \\).\n[5] StandardModel.HiggsField.Potential.isMaxOn_iff_isMinOn_neg  (theorem)\n    (φ : HiggsField) (x : SpaceTime) :\n  IsMaxOn (fun (φ, x) => P.toFun φ x) Set.univ (φ, x) ↔ IsMinOn (fun (φ, x) => P.neg.toFun φ x) Set.univ (φ, x)\n    For a Higgs field \\( \\phi \\) and a spacetime point \\( x \\), the condition that the Higgs potential function \\( P.toFun \\) has a maximum at \\( (\\phi, x) \\) is equivalent to the condition that the negated Higgs potential function \\( P.neg.toFun \\) has a minimum at the same point.\n[6] Electromagnetism.DistElectromagneticPotential.isExterma_iff_tensor  (theorem)\n    {𝓕 : FreeSpace} (A : DistElectromagneticPotential d) (J : DistLorentzCurrentDensity d) :\n  IsExtrema 𝓕 A J ↔ ∀ ε, {((1 / 𝓕.μ₀ : ℝ) • distTensorDeriv A.fieldStrength ε| κ κ ν')+-(J ε| ν')}ᵀ = 0\n    For a distributional electromagnetic potential \\( A \\) and a distributional Lorentz current density \\( J \\), the condition that \\( A \\) is an extremum of the Lagrangian is equivalent to the variational gradient of the Lagrangian \\( A.gradLagrangian \\) being equal to zero when evaluated with respect to a free space \\( 𝓕 \\) and the current density \\( J \\).\n[7] Space.radialAngularMeasure_integrable_pow_neg_two  (theorem)\n    {d : ℕ} : Integrable (fun x : Space d => (1 + ‖x‖) ^ (-(d + 1) : ℝ)) radialAngularMeasure\n    For any natural number $d$, the function $x \\mapsto (1 + \\|x\\|)^{-(d + 1)}$ is integrable with respect to the radial angular measure on the space $\\text{Space} \\ d$.\n[8] Electromagnetism.DistElectromagneticPotential.oneDimPointParticle_isExterma  (theorem)\n    (𝓕 : FreeSpace) (q : ℝ) (r₀ : Space 1) :\n  (oneDimPointParticle 𝓕 q r₀).IsExtrema 𝓕 (oneDimPointParticleCurrentDensity 𝓕.c q r₀)\n    For a distributional electromagnetic potential \\( A \\) associated with a point particle in one-dimensional space, the condition that \\( A \\) is an extremum of the Lagrangian is equivalent to the variational gradient of the Lagrangian \\( A.gradLagrangian \\) being equal to zero when evaluated with respect to a free space \\( 𝓕 \\) and a distributional Lorentz current density \\( J \\).\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"web_search\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"single slit diffraction intensity formula I(theta) = I0 (sin beta / beta)^2 first minimum maximum\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n\n# Round 1 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nLean compiler reported errors: unexpected token '('; expected command\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Incorrect\n\n</round>\n\n# Round 2 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"physsearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"single slit diffraction intensity pattern wavelength\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] QuantumMechanics.OneDimension.HilbertSpace.planewaveFunctional  (definition)\n    (k : ℝ) : 𝓢(ℝ, ℂ) →L[ℂ] ℂ\n    The function maps a real number \\( k \\) to the plane wave represented as the tempered distribution \\( \\exp(2\\pi i k x) \\), which is a member of the dual of the Schwartz submodule of the Hilbert space.\n[2] ClassicalMechanics.transverseHarmonicPlaneWave  (definition)\n    (k : WaveVector) (f₀x f₀y ω δx δy : ℝ) (hk : k = EuclideanSpace.single 2 (ω / c)) :\n  Time → Space → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 3)\n    This definition describes a transverse monochromatic time-harmonic plane wave, where the direction of propagation is given by \\( \\text{EuclideanSpace.single} \\ 2 \\ 1 \\). The amplitudes are represented by \\( f₀x \\) and \\( f₀y \\), while \\( \\omega \\) denotes the angular frequency. The phases for the respective components \\( fx \\) and \\( fy \\) are given by \\( \\delta x \\) and \\( \\delta y \\).\n[3] Electromagnetism.ElectromagneticPotential.harmonicWaveX  (definition)\n    (𝓕 : FreeSpace) (k : ℝ) (E₀ : Fin d → ℝ) (φ : Fin d → ℝ) : ElectromagneticPotential d.succ\n    The electromagnetic potential for a harmonic wave traveling in the \\( x \\)-direction is defined with wave number \\( k \\).\n[4] Electromagnetism.ElectromagneticPotential.IsPlaneWave.electricFunction  (definition)\n    {d : ℕ} {𝓕 : FreeSpace} {A : ElectromagneticPotential d} {s : Direction d} (hA : IsPlaneWave 𝓕 A s) :\n  ℝ → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)\n    The function maps a real number \\( t \\) to the corresponding electric field of a plane wave in the Euclidean space \\( \\mathbb{R}^d \\).\n[5] ClassicalMechanics.harmonicWave  (definition)\n    (a g : ℝ → Space d → ℝ) (ω : WaveVector d → ℝ) (k : WaveVector d) : Time → Space d → ℝ\n    The function describes a time-harmonic wave in terms of angular frequency \\( \\omega \\) and wave vector \\( k \\). Specifically, it takes a time \\( t \\) and a position in space \\( r \\) to return the wave's amplitude, which is given by the product of a function evaluated at \\( \\omega k \\) and the cosine of the expression \\( \\omega k \\cdot t - g(\\omega k) r \\).\n[6] Electromagnetism.ElectromagneticPotential.harmonicWaveX_vectorPotential_succ  (theorem)\n    {d} (𝓕 : FreeSpace) (k : ℝ) (E₀ : Fin d → ℝ) (φ : Fin d → ℝ) (t : Time) (x : Space d.succ) (i : Fin d) :\n  (harmonicWaveX 𝓕 k E₀ φ).vectorPotential 𝓕.c t x i.succ =\n    -E₀ i * 1 / (𝓕.c * k) * Real.sin (k * (t.val * 𝓕.c - x 0) + φ i)\n    The electromagnetic potential for a harmonic wave traveling in the \\( x \\)-direction is defined with wave number \\( k \\).\n[7] QuantumMechanics.OneDimension.HilbertSpace.planewaveFunctional_apply  (theorem)\n    (k : ℝ) (ψ : 𝓢(ℝ, ℂ)) : planewaveFunctional k ψ = 𝓕 ψ k\n    The function maps a real number \\( k \\) to the plane wave represented as the tempered distribution \\( \\exp(2\\pi i k x) \\), which is a member of the dual of the Schwartz submodule of the Hilbert space.\n[8] ClassicalMechanics.WaveEquation  (definition)\n    {d} (f : Time → Space d → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)) (t : Time) (x : Space d) (c : ℝ) : Prop\n    The general form of the wave equation describes how a function \\( f \\) propagates through space and time, where \\( c \\) represents the propagation speed.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"web_search\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"single slit diffraction intensity formula I(theta) = I0 (sin(beta)/beta)^2 beta = pi w sin(theta) / lambda\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Intensity of Diffraction Pattern\n    The intensity of nth secondary maximum diffraction pattern is I = I0 (sin β / β)^2 ; where I0 is the intensity of central maximum … φ = 2π /λ. ...\n    https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tMMKHRX7vjc\n[2] 4.3: Intensity in Single-Slit Diffraction\n    To calculate the intensity of the diffraction pattern, we follow the phasor method used for calculations with ac circuits in Alternating-Current Circuits.\n    https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/University_Physics/University_Physics_(OpenStax)/University_Physics_III_-_Optics_and_Modern_Physics_(OpenStax)/04%3A_Diffraction/4.03%3A_Intensity_in_Single-Slit_Diffraction\n[3] Solved The intensity of light in the Fraunhofer diffraction\n    The intensity of light in the Fraunhofer diffraction pattern of a single slit is I = I_0 (sin beta/beta)^2, where beta = pi a sin theta/lambda. Show ...\n    https://www.chegg.com/homework-help/questions-and-answers/intensity-light-fraunhofer-diffraction-pattern-single-slit-i0-sin-beta-beta-2-beta-pi-sin--q18269038\n[4] Intensity of single slit diffraction\n    θ λ = 2πD sin θ. I(θ) = I0 sin(β/2) β/2 2 , where β ≡ 2πD sinθ λ (4) which 2 sin. Eθ = λE0 πD sinθsin ωt + πD sin θ λ\n    https://scipp-legacy.pbsci.ucsc.edu/~haber/ph5B/oneslit09.pdf\n[5] Intensity at θ=0 in slit diffraction\n    From the explanation on diffraction at http://web.mit.edu/viz/EM/visualizations/coursenotes/modules/guide14.pdf I=I0[sin(β/2)β/2]2=I0[sin(πasinθ/λ)πasinθ ...\n    https://physics.stackexchange.com/questions/341127/intensity-at-theta-0-in-slit-diffraction\n</tool_response>\nBased on the search results, I can now formalize the single-slit diffraction problem. The key formula is:\n- Intensity: $I(\\theta) = I_0 \\left(\\frac{\\sin\\beta}{\\beta}\\right)^2$ where $\\beta = \\frac{\\pi w \\sin\\theta}{\\lambda}$\n- First minimum occurs when $\\beta = \\pi$ (i.e., $w \\sin\\theta = \\lambda$)\n- First maximum (secondary) occurs approximately when $\\beta \\approx 3\\pi/2$ (i.e., $w \\sin\\theta \\approx 3\\lambda/2$)\n\nGiven $w = 5\\lambda$, I need to find:\n1. The intensity as a function of angle\n2. First minimum position: $\\sin\\theta = \\lambda/w = 1/5$\n3. First maximum estimate: $\\sin\\theta \\approx 3\\lambda/(2w) = 3/10$\n\n```lean4\nimport Physlib\nimport Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Trigonometric.Basic\n\nopen Real\n\n/--\nSingle-slit diffraction problem.\nGiven monochromatic light of wavelength λ incident on a slit of width w = 5λ.\nThe intensity pattern is I(θ) = I₀ * (sin(β)/β)² where β = π*w*sin(θ)/λ.\nWe compute:\n1. The intensity as a function of angle θ\n2. The position of the first minimum\n3. An estimate of the first secondary maximum position\n-/\ntheorem single_slit_diffraction_pattern \n  (λ : ℝ) (hλ : λ > 0) \n  (w : ℝ) (hw : w = 5 * λ)\n  (I₀ : ℝ) (hI₀ : I₀ > 0) :\n  let β : ℝ → ℝ := fun θ => (π * w * sin θ) / λ\n  let I : ℝ → ℝ := fun θ => I₀ * ((sin (β θ)) / (β θ))^2\n  let first_min_sin : ℝ := λ / w\n  let first_max_sin_approx : ℝ := (3 * λ) / (2 * w)\n  -- First minimum occurs when β = π, i.e., sin θ = λ/w = 1/5\n  (β (Real.arcsin first_min_sin) = π) ∧\n  -- First secondary maximum approximately when β ≈ 3π/2, i.e., sin θ ≈ 3λ/(2w) = 3/10\n  (abs (β (Real.arcsin first_max_sin_approx) - (3 * π / 2)) < π / 10) ∧\n  -- Given w = 5λ, the specific values\n  (first_min_sin = 1/5) ∧\n  (first_max_sin_approx = 3/10) := by sorry\n```\n\n[syntax_rewrite] `λ` → `fun`; unicode subscript digits → ASCII\n```lean4\nimport Physlib\nimport Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Trigonometric.Basic\n\nopen Real\n\n/--\nSingle-slit diffraction problem.\nGiven monochromatic light of wavelength fun incident on a slit of width w = 5λ.\nThe intensity pattern is I(θ) = I0 * (sin(β)/β)² where β = π*w*sin(θ)/λ.\nWe compute:\n1. The intensity as a function of angle θ\n2. The position of the first minimum\n3. An estimate of the first secondary maximum position\n-/\ntheorem single_slit_diffraction_pattern \n  (fun : ℝ) (hλ : fun > 0) \n  (w : ℝ) (hw : w = 5 * λ)\n  (I0 : ℝ) (hI0 : I0 > 0) :\n  let β : ℝ → ℝ := fun θ => (π * w * sin θ) / fun let I : ℝ → ℝ := fun θ => I0 * ((sin (β θ)) / (β θ))^2\n  let first_min_sin : ℝ := fun / w\n  let first_max_sin_approx : ℝ := (3 * λ) / (2 * w)\n  -- First minimum occurs when β = π, i.e., sin θ = λ/w = 1/5\n  (β (Real.arcsin first_min_sin) = π) ∧\n  -- First secondary maximum approximately when β ≈ 3π/2, i.e., sin θ ≈ 3λ/(2w) = 3/10\n  (abs (β (Real.arcsin first_max_sin_approx) - (3 * π / 2)) < π / 10) ∧\n  -- Given w = 5λ, the specific values\n  (first_min_sin = 1/5) ∧\n  (first_max_sin_approx = 3/10) := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 2 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nLean compiler reported errors: unexpected token 'fun'; expected '_' or identifier\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Incorrect\n\n</round>\n\n# Round 3 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"physsearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"single slit diffraction intensity pattern\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] QuantumMechanics.OneDimension.HilbertSpace.planewaveFunctional  (definition)\n    (k : ℝ) : 𝓢(ℝ, ℂ) →L[ℂ] ℂ\n    The function maps a real number \\( k \\) to the plane wave represented as the tempered distribution \\( \\exp(2\\pi i k x) \\), which is a member of the dual of the Schwartz submodule of the Hilbert space.\n[2] Electromagnetism.ElectromagneticPotential.harmonicWaveX  (definition)\n    (𝓕 : FreeSpace) (k : ℝ) (E₀ : Fin d → ℝ) (φ : Fin d → ℝ) : ElectromagneticPotential d.succ\n    The electromagnetic potential for a harmonic wave traveling in the \\( x \\)-direction is defined with wave number \\( k \\).\n[3] QuantumMechanics.OneDimension.HilbertSpace.planewaveFunctional_apply  (theorem)\n    (k : ℝ) (ψ : 𝓢(ℝ, ℂ)) : planewaveFunctional k ψ = 𝓕 ψ k\n    The function maps a real number \\( k \\) to the plane wave represented as the tempered distribution \\( \\exp(2\\pi i k x) \\), which is a member of the dual of the Schwartz submodule of the Hilbert space.\n[4] Physlib.Distribution.fourierTransform_apply  (theorem)\n    (u : E→d[ℂ] F) (η : 𝓢(E, ℂ)) : u.fourierTransform E F η = u (fourierTransformCLM ℂ η)\n    The Fourier transform of a distribution \\( u \\) is denoted as \\( F\\{u\\} \\) and is defined such that for a test function \\( \\eta \\), the Fou