n      { toFluidState := toFluidState_1, stress := stress_1, bodyForce := bodyForce_1 } →\n    toFluidState = toFluidState_1 ∧ stress = stress_1 ∧ bodyForce = bodyForce_1\n    This constructor creates an instance of the inductive type \\( \\text{FluidInMomentumBalance} \\ d \\) by taking a natural number \\( d \\), a fluid state of type \\( \\text{FluidState} \\ d \\), a stress tensor of type \\( \\text{StressTensor} \\ d \\), and a body-force field of type \\( \\text{BodyForce} \\ d \\).\n[5] DimPressure.pascal  (definition)\n    : DimPressure\n    The dimensional pressure corresponding to 1 pascal, Pa.\n[6] NVEHamiltonian.pressure  (definition)\n    (T : ℝ) : ℝ\n    The pressure is defined as a function of temperature \\( T \\), serving as the conjugate variable to volume.\n[7] DimPressure.standardAtmosphere  (definition)\n    : DimPressure\n    The dimensional pressure corresponding to 1 standard atmosphere is defined as 101,325 pascals.\n[8] DimPressure  (abbrev)\n    : Type\n    Pressure is defined as a dimensional quantity with dimension \\( ML^{-1}T^{-2} \\).\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"physsearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"gravitational field infinite slab sheet mass density\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] FluidDynamics.MassDensity  (abbrev)\n    (d : ℕ)\n    A mass density field defined on a \\(d\\)-dimensional space.\n[2] Electromagnetism.DistElectromagneticPotential.infiniteWire_electricField  (theorem)\n    (𝓕 : FreeSpace) (I : ℝ) : (infiniteWire 𝓕 I).electricField 𝓕.c = 0\n    For any free space \\( \\mathcal{F} \\) and current \\( I \\) flowing through an infinite wire, the electric field generated by this wire is equal to zero.\n[3] FluidDynamics.BodyForce  (abbrev)\n    (d : ℕ)\n    A body-force field per unit mass defined in a \\(d\\)-dimensional space.\n[4] Electromagnetism.DistElectromagneticPotential.wireCurrentDensity_currentDensity_thrd  (theorem)\n    (c : SpeedOfLight) (I : ℝ) (ε : 𝓢(Time × Space 3, ℝ)) : (wireCurrentDensity c I).currentDensity c ε 2 = 0\n    For an infinite wire carrying a current \\( I \\) along the \\( x \\)-axis, the associated current density is defined as the flow of electric charge per unit area in a given direction.\n[5] Electromagnetism.DistElectromagneticPotential.infiniteWire  (definition)\n    (𝓕 : FreeSpace) (I : ℝ) : DistElectromagneticPotential 3\n    The electromagnetic potential generated by an infinite wire aligned along the x-axis carrying a current \\( I \\).\n[6] Electromagnetism.DistElectromagneticPotential.wireCurrentDensity  (definition)\n    (c : SpeedOfLight) : ℝ →ₗ[ℝ] DistLorentzCurrentDensity 3\n    The current density associated with an infinite wire carrying a current \\( I \\) along the \\( x \\)-axis.\n[7] Electromagnetism.DistElectromagneticPotential.wireCurrentDensity_chargeDesnity  (theorem)\n    (c : SpeedOfLight) (I : ℝ) : (wireCurrentDensity c I).chargeDensity c = 0\n    For any speed of light \\( c \\) and current \\( I \\) associated with an infinite wire, the charge density of the wire current density is equal to zero, i.e., \\( (wireCurrentDensity(c, I)).chargeDensity(c) = 0 \\).\n[8] Electromagnetism.ChargeDensity  (abbrev)\n    Type\n    The charge density is a function that maps a time and a space to a real number, representing the distribution of electric charge in a given region.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"web_search\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"gravitational field infinite slab sheet surface density sigma formula 4*pi*G\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Physics 18.1 Gravity with Mass Distribution (10 of 16) Gauss' Law ...\n    In this video I will calculate the force of gravity on mass m from an infinite sheet of mass using Gauss' Law for Gravitation.\n    https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5Z8gbUYdIdQ\n[2] Gravity Anomaly Due to a Thin Finite Horizontal Plate\n    The gravity due to an infinite plate (also called an infinite Bouguer slab) can be expressed as, $$g_z = 2 \\pi G \\Delta \\rho h$$. [Top]. Model assumptions. This ...\n    https://gscommunitycodes.usf.edu/geoscicommunitycodes/public/geophysics/Gravity/semi-infinite-slab.php\n[3] Gravitational force and potential in infite slab\n    Let's say that we have an infinite slab of height 2h and mass density ρ. Let's define x,y as the axis parallel to the slab and z as the perpendicular one, ...\n    https://physics.stackexchange.com/questions/215110/gravitational-force-and-potential-in-infite-slab\n[4] Electric Field for an Infinite Sheet\n    How do we find the electric field for an infinite sheet charge? In this video, we use Gauss' law to find this quantity, and we show that the ...\n    https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U2UL_o0qjUg\n[5] (PDF) The gravitational field of an infinite flat slab\n    We treat infinitely thin slabs in full detail and indicate how thick slabs can increase the variety of external geometry pairs. gravitational field equations\n    https://www.researchgate.net/publication/278741137_The_gravitational_field_of_an_infinite_flat_slab\n</tool_response>\nBased on the physics analysis and the provided options, the correct answer is **B**, which derives $p(0) = 2\\pi G \\sigma^2$ using Gauss's law for gravity and hydrostatic equilibrium. The key steps are:\n1. Use Gauss's law for an infinite slab to find the gravitational field $g(z) = 4\\pi G \\int_0^z \\rho(z') dz'$.\n2. Apply hydrostatic equilibrium: $dp/dz = -\\rho(z) g(z)$.\n3. Integrate from $z=0$ to $z=h$ with boundary condition $p(h)=0$.\n4. The result is $p(0) = 2\\pi G \\sigma^2$ where $\\sigma = \\int_0^h \\rho(z) dz$.\n\nNow I'll formalize this in Lean 4.\n\n```lean4\nimport Physlib\nimport Mathlib.Analysis.Calculus.FDeriv.Basic\nimport Mathlib.Analysis.Calculus.IntegralDeriv\nimport Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral\n\nopen Real Set MeasureTheory\n\n/--\nConsider an infinite flat slab of thickness 2h, symmetric about z=0, with density ρ(z) \ndepending only on z. The slab is in hydrostatic equilibrium under its own gravity.\nLet σ = ∫₀ʰ ρ(z) dz be the surface density (mass per unit area of half the slab).\nThe pressure at the midplane z=0 is given by p(0) = 2πGσ².\n-/\ntheorem slab_midplane_pressure \n  (G : ℝ) (h : ℝ) (ρ : ℝ → ℝ) (p : ℝ → ℝ)\n  (hG : G > 0) (hh : h > 0)\n  (hρ_nonneg : ∀ z ∈ Icc 0 h, ρ z ≥ 0)\n  (hρ_symm : ∀ z, ρ (-z) = ρ z)\n  (hσ_def : let σ := ∫ z in (0)..h, ρ z; True)\n  (hg_field : ∀ z ∈ Icc 0 h, \n    let g_z := 4 * Real.pi * G * ∫ z' in (0)..z, ρ z'\n    ∀ z' ∈ Icc 0 h, HasDerivAt p (-ρ z' * g_z) z')\n  (hp_boundary : p h = 0) :\n  p 0 = 2 * Real.pi * G * (∫ z in (0)..h, ρ z)^2 := by sorry\n```\n\n[syntax_rewrite] unicode subscript digits → ASCII\n```lean4\nimport Physlib\nimport Mathlib.Analysis.Calculus.FDeriv.Basic\nimport Mathlib.Analysis.Calculus.IntegralDeriv\nimport Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral\n\nopen Real Set MeasureTheory\n\n/--\nConsider an infinite flat slab of thickness 2h, symmetric about z=0, with density ρ(z) \ndepending only on z. The slab is in hydrostatic equilibrium under its own gravity.\nLet σ = ∫0ʰ ρ(z) dz be the surface density (mass per unit area of half the slab).\nThe pressure at the midplane z=0 is given by p(0) = 2πGσ².\n-/\ntheorem slab_midplane_pressure \n  (G : ℝ) (h : ℝ) (ρ : ℝ → ℝ) (p : ℝ → ℝ)\n  (hG : G > 0) (hh : h > 0)\n  (hρ_nonneg : ∀ z ∈ Icc 0 h, ρ z ≥ 0)\n  (hρ_symm : ∀ z, ρ (-z) = ρ z)\n  (hσ_def : let σ := ∫ z in (0)..h, ρ z; True)\n  (hg_field : ∀ z ∈ Icc 0 h, \n    let g_z := 4 * Real.pi * G * ∫ z' in (0)..z, ρ z'\n    ∀ z' ∈ Icc 0 h, HasDerivAt p (-ρ z' * g_z) z')\n  (hp_boundary : p h = 0) :\n  p 0 = 2 * Real.pi * G * (∫ z in (0)..h, ρ z)^2 := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 2 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nYour brief analysis:\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: The problem requires finding the midplane pressure $p(0)$ for an infinite self-gravitating slab of thickness $2h$ with density $\\rho(z)$. The physics involves hydrostatic equilibrium ($dp/dz = -\\rho(z)g(z)$) and Gauss's law for gravity ($g(z) = 4\\pi G \\int_0^z \\rho(z') dz'$). The boundary condition is $p(h)=0$. The correct result (derived in Option B of the text) is $p(0) = 2\\pi G \\sigma^2$, where $\\sigma = \\int_0^h \\rho(z) dz$.\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored): The theorem conclusion `p 0 = 2 * Real.pi * G * (∫ z in (0)..h, ρ z)^2` matches the correct physical result. However, the hypothesis `hg_field` contains a logical scoping error: it defines `g_z` using an outer quantifier `z` and applies it to the derivative condition for all `z'` (`∀ z, let g_z := ...; ∀ z', HasDerivAt p (-ρ z' * g_z) z'`). This implies the gravitational field is constant across the slab, which contradicts the integral definition of `g_z` unless $\\rho=0$. Additionally, `hσ_def` is vacuous (`True`), and the code uses `let` inside theorem argument types, violating the constraint \"no let inside the theorem type\".\n3. Comparative Verification: While the conclusion matches the mathematical result, the premises fail to faithfully encode the physical laws. The `hg_field` hypothesis is mathematically inconsistent with the intended model (self-gravity varies with depth) for any non-zero density. The syntax also violates specific constraints provided in the instructions.\n4. Conclusion: The Lean statement is Incorrect.\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: Mismatches: 1) `hg_field` has incorrect variable scoping, logically forcing constant gravity and invalidating the model for $\\rho \\neq 0$. 2) `hσ_def` is a vacuous hypothesis. 3) Syntax violation: `let` used inside theorem type arguments.\n\nResult: Incorrect\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Incorrect\n\n</round>\n\n# Round 3 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"physsearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"hydrostatic equilibrium infinite slab self gravity pressure midplane\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] FluidDynamics.FluidInMomentumBalanc