ch?v=x8gnQhM3fJY\n[2] Electrodynamics: Magnetic Field due to a Rotating Charged ...\n    Use the result of Ex. 5.6 to calculate the magnetic field at the center of a uniformly charged spherical shell, of radius R and total charge Q, ...\n    https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n5vrZvJ6Wo0\n[3] P. LeClair\n    the magnetic field at the center of a circular loop of radius r carrying a current I is B = μoI 2r (loop radius r) This is easily derived from ...\n    https://pleclair.ua.edu/ph126/Homework/HW5_SOLN.pdf\n[4] What is the magnetic field created by a charged square ...\n    Homework-like questions and check-my-work questions are considered off-topic here, particularly when asking about specific computations instead of underlying ...\n    https://physics.stackexchange.com/questions/818483/what-is-the-magnetic-field-created-by-a-charged-square-rotating-about-its-center\n[5] Magnetic Field Due to a Spinning Ring of Charge\n    Prompt: \"This is a ring with radius R R and total charge Q Q and spinning with period T T . Find a formula for the magnetic field →B B → due to this ring that ...\n    https://paradigms.oregonstate.edu/act/2067\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"web_search\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"magnetic field center rotating charged ring B = mu0 * lambda * omega / 2 derivation\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Electrodynamics: Magnetic Field due to a Rotating Charged Sphere\n    From Griffiths chapter 5 Use the result of Ex. 5.6 to calculate the magnetic field at the center of a uniformly charged spherical shell, ...\n    https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n5vrZvJ6Wo0\n[2] Magnetic Field Produced by a Spinning Disk of Charge - YouTube\n    Physics Ninja calculates the magnetic field produced by a spinning disk of charge Q. The charge is uniformly spread on the surface of the ...\n    https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x8gnQhM3fJY\n[3] What is the magnetic field created by a charged square rotating about its ...\n    Now, this contraption is made to spin about the center of the square with an angular velocity ω. The linear charge density of every rod is λ.\n    https://physics.stackexchange.com/questions/818483/what-is-the-magnetic-field-created-by-a-charged-square-rotating-about-its-center\n[4] A ring of mass m and radius R is given a charge q. It is then rotated about ...\n    To find the magnetic field produced at the center of a rotating charged ring, We need to find the magnetic field B at the center of the ring. ### Step 2: Use ...\n    https://allen.in/dn/qna/318308955\n[5] A ring of radius R rotates with frequency about its own axis . ... - Filo\n    Substitute the current into the magnetic field formula: B=2Rμ0​I​=2Rμ0​λ(2πfR)​=μ0​λπf. Final Answer: B = \\mu_0 \\lambda \\pi f. Ask your next ...\n    https://askfilo.com/user-question-answers-smart-solutions/a-ring-of-radius-r-rotates-with-frequency-about-its-own-axis-3231393839333638\n</tool_response>\nBased on the physics problem, I need to formalize the statement about finding the magnetic field at the center of a rotating charged ring.\n\nFrom the physics:\n- A ring of radius R with uniform linear charge density λ (λ > 0)\n- Rotating about an axis perpendicular to the ring plane through its center with angular velocity ω\n- We need to find the magnetic field B₀ at the center O\n\nThe equivalent current due to rotation: I = λv = λ(ωR) where v = ωR is the tangential velocity.\nActually, more precisely: the current I = (charge passing per unit time) = λ × (circumference) × (frequency) = λ × 2πR × (ω/2π) = λωR\n\nFor a circular loop carrying current I, the magnetic field at center is: B = μ₀I/(2R)\n\nSubstituting: B₀ = μ₀(λωR)/(2R) = μ₀λω/2\n\nLet me formalize this in Lean 4:\n\n```lean4\nimport Physlib\n\n/-- \nMagnetic field at the center of a uniformly charged rotating ring.\nGiven a ring of radius R with linear charge density λ > 0 rotating with angular velocity ω\nabout an axis perpendicular to the ring plane through its center, the magnetic field B₀\nat the center is μ₀λω/2, directed along the axis of rotation.\n-/\ntheorem magnetic_field_rotating_charged_ring (R : ℝ) (hR : R > 0) (lambda : ℝ) (hlam : lambda > 0) \n    (omega : ℝ) (homega : omega > 0) : \n    ∃ (B0 : ℝ), B0 = (Real.pi * 2 * 1e-7) * lambda * omega / 2 := by sorry\n```\n\n[syntax_rewrite] `λ` → `fun`; unicode subscript digits → ASCII\n```lean4\nimport Physlib\n\n/-- \nMagnetic field at the center of a uniformly charged rotating ring.\nGiven a ring of radius R with linear charge density fun > 0 rotating with angular velocity ω\nabout an axis perpendicular to the ring plane through its center, the magnetic field B0\nat the center is μ0λω/2, directed along the axis of rotation.\n-/\ntheorem magnetic_field_rotating_charged_ring (R : ℝ) (hR : R > 0) (lambda : ℝ) (hlam : lambda > 0) \n    (omega : ℝ) (homega : omega > 0) : \n    ∃ (B0 : ℝ), B0 = (Real.pi * 2 * 1e-7) * lambda * omega / 2 := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 2 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nYour brief analysis:\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: The problem asks for the magnetic field $\\boldsymbol{B}_{0}$ at the center of a uniformly charged ring (radius $R$, linear charge density $\\lambda > 0$) rotating with angular velocity $\\omega$ about its central perpendicular axis. The physics derivation yields current $I = \\lambda R \\omega$, and the magnetic field at the center is $B = \\frac{\\mu_0 I}{2R} = \\frac{\\mu_0 \\lambda \\omega}{2}$. Using $\\mu_0 = 4\\pi \\times 10^{-7} \\, \\text{T}\\cdot\\text{m/A}$, the magnitude is $B_0 = 2\\pi \\times 10^{-7} \\lambda \\omega$.\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored): The theorem declares parameters $R, \\lambda, \\omega$ with positivity constraints. The conclusion asserts the existence of a real number `B0` equal to `(Real.pi * 2 * 1e-7) * lambda * omega / 2`. This expression simplifies to $\\pi \\times 10^{-7} \\lambda \\omega$. The variable `R` is declared but not used in the formula.\n3. Comparative Verification: \n   - **Formula Mismatch:** The Lean expression evaluates to $\\pi \\times 10^{-7} \\lambda \\omega$, which is half the correct physical value ($2\\pi \\times 10^{-7} \\lambda \\omega$). The Lean code effectively uses $\\mu_0/2$ instead of $\\mu_0$ in the numerator, then divides by 2 again.\n   - **Variable `R`:** While `R` cancels out in the physics derivation, its absence in the Lean formula is mathematically consistent with the derived result, though it might be unexpected formally.\n   - **Vector vs Scalar:** The problem asks for a vector $\\boldsymbol{B}_{0}$, but Lean provides a scalar magnitude. Per guidelines, this is acceptable for \"find X\" problems.\n   - **Image Description Mismatch:** The prompt's image description describes an electrical circuit, which contradicts the text (rotating ring). The analysis follows the text.\n4. Conclusion: The Lean statement is mathematically incorrect due to the wrong numerical coefficient in the magnetic field formula.\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: The Lean formula `(Real.pi * 2 * 1e-7) * lambda * omega / 2` results in $\\pi \\times 10^{-7} \\lambda \\omega$, whereas the correct physics formula is $2\\pi \\times 10^{-7} \\lambda \\omega$. This factor-of-2 error makes the formalization incorrect.\n\nResult: Incorrect\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Incorrect\n\n</round>\n\n# Round 3 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"physsearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"vacuum permeability mu_0 constant Physlib\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Electromagnetism.FreeSpace.μ₀_nonneg  (theorem)\n    : 0 ≤ 𝓕.μ₀\n    The magnetic permeability of free space, denoted as \\( \\mu_0 \\), is a real number that characterizes the ability of free space to support magnetic field lines.\n[2] Electromagnetism.FreeSpace.μ₀  (definition)\n    Electromagnetism.FreeSpace → ℝ\n    The function maps the inductive type representing free space in electromagnetism to its magnetic permeability, denoted as \\( \\mu_0 \\), which is a real number.\n[3] Electromagnetism.FreeSpace.μ₀_pos  (theorem)\n    ∀ (self : Electromagnetism.FreeSpace), 0 < self.μ₀\n    For any instance of the inductive type representing free space in electromagnetism, the magnetic permeability \\( \\mu_0 \\) is strictly greater than zero, i.e., \\( 0 < \\mu_0 \\).\n[4] Electromagnetism.FreeSpace.μ₀_ne_zero  (theorem)\n    : 𝓕.μ₀ ≠ 0\n    The magnetic permeability of free space, denoted as \\( \\mu_0 \\), is a non-zero real number.\n[5] Electromagnetism.EMSystem.μ₀  (definition)\n    Electromagnetism.EMSystem → ℝ\n    An inductive type representing an electromagnetic system.\n</tool_response>\n```lean4\nimport Physlib\n\n/-- \nMagnetic field at the center of a uniformly charged rotating ring.\nGiven a ring of radius R with linear charge density λ > 0 rotating with angular velocity ω\nabout an axis perpendicular to the ring plane through its center, the magnetic field B₀\nat the center has magnitude μ₀λω/2 = 2π × 10⁻⁷ λω.\n-/\ntheorem magnetic_field_rotating_charged_ring (R : ℝ) (hR : R > 0) (lambda : ℝ) (hlam : lambda > 0) \n    (omega : ℝ) (homega : omega > 0) : \n    ∃ (B0 : ℝ), B0 = 2 * Real.pi * 1e-7 * lambda * omega := by sorry\n```\n\n[syntax_rewrite] `λ` → `fun`; unicode subscript digits → ASCII\n```lean4\nimport Physlib\n\n/-- \nMagnetic field at the center of a uniformly charged rotating ring.\nGiven a ring of radius R with linear charge density fun > 0 rotating with angular velocity ω\nabout an axis perpendicular to the ring plane through its center, the magnetic field B0\nat the center has magnitude μ0λω/2 = 2π × 10⁻⁷ λω.\n-/\ntheorem magnetic_field_rotating_charged_ring (R : ℝ) (hR : R > 0) (lambda : ℝ) (hlam : lambda > 0) \n    (omega : ℝ) (homega : omega > 0) : \n    ∃ (B0 : ℝ), B0 = 2 * Real.pi * 1e-7 * lambda * omega := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 3 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nYour brief analysis:\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: The problem asks for the magnetic field magnitude $B_0$ at the center of a uniformly charged ring (radius $R$, linear charge density $\\lambda > 0$) rotating with angular velocity $\\omega$. The current is $I = \\lambda R \\omega$. The magnetic field at the center of a current loop is $B = \\frac{\\mu_0 I}{2R}$. Substituting $I$ yields $B_0 = \\frac{\\mu_0 \\lambda \\omega}{2}$. Using $\\mu_0 = 4\\pi \\times 10^{-7} \\, \\text{T}\\cdot\\text{m/A}$, the magnitude is $B_0 = 2\\pi \\times 10^{-7} \\lambda \\omega$.\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored): The theorem declares parameters `R`, `lambda`, `omega` as positive reals. The conclusion asserts the existence of a real `B0` equal to `2 * Real.pi * 1e-7 * lambda * omega`. The variable `R` is declared but does not appear in the final formula (consistent with physics derivation where $R$ cancels).\n3. Comparative Verification: \n   - **Formula:** The Lean expression `2 * Real.pi * 1e-7 * lambda * omega` corresponds exactly to the derived physical formula $2\\pi \\times 10^{-7} \\lambda \\omega$. The previous error (extra division by 2) has been corrected.\n   - **Variables:** All physical parameters ($\\lambda, \\omega$) are present and constrained correctly ($\\lambda, \\omega > 0$). `R` is correctly included in the setup hypotheses despite cancelling out in the result.\n   - **Image/Text Mismatch:** The prompt's image description describes an electrical circuit, which contradicts the text (rotating ring). The Lean statement correctly follows the text description.\n   - **Scalar vs Vector:** The problem asks for vector $\\boldsymbol{B}_0$, but Lean provides scalar magnitude. Per guidelines, this is acceptable for \"find X\" problems.\n   - **Types:** The literal `1e-7` is technically a `Float` in Lean 4, while other terms are `Real`. While this requires coercion in strict Lean, semantically it represents the correct constant $10^{-7}$ in this context.\n4. Conclusion: The Lean statement is a correct and exact formalization of the mathematical problem described in the text.\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: The formula in the Lean statement matches the physics derivation ($2\\pi \\times 10^{-7} \\lambda \\omega$). The constraints on variables are appropriate. The previous inconsistency (incorrect coefficient) is resolved.\n\nResult: Correct\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Correct\n\n</round>\n\n</think>\n\n<answer>\n\n```lean4\nimport Physlib\n\n/-- \nMagnetic field at the center of a uniformly charged rotating ring.\nGiven a ring of radius R with linear charge density fun > 0 rotating with angular velocity ω\nabout an axis perpendicular to the ring plane through its center, the magnetic field B0\nat the center has magnitude μ0λω/2 = 2π × 10⁻⁷ λω.\n-/\ntheorem magnetic_field_rotating_charged_ring (R : ℝ) (hR : R > 0) (lambda : ℝ) (hlam : lambda > 0) \n    (omega : ℝ) (homega : omega > 0) : \n    ∃ (B0 : ℝ), B0 = 2 * Real.pi * 1e-7 * lambda * omega := by sorry\n```\n\n</answer>"}], "informal_statement": "如习题 8－20图所示，半径为 $R$ ，电荷线密度为 $\\lambda(\\lambda>0)$ 的均匀带电圆环，绕圆心且与圆平面垂直的轴以角速度 $\\omega$ 转动，求：圆心 $O$ 处的磁感应强度 $\\boldsymbol{B}_{0}$ 。\n\n[Image description: IMAGE 1:\nSchematic diagram of a multi-loop electrical circuit containing two batteries with emfs $\\varepsilon_1$ (internal resistance $r_1$) and $\\varepsilon_2$ (internal resistance $r_2$), five resistors ($R_1$, $R_2$ in parallel between nodes $A$ and $B$; $R_3$, $R_4$ in series; and $R_4$ between nodes $C$ and $D$), with all circuit nodes ($A$, $B$, $C$, $D$) labeled.]", "num_rounds": 3, "num_tool_calls": 7, "tools_used": ["physsearch", "web_search"], "success": true, "syntax_pass": true, "syntax_rewrites": ["`λ` → `fun`", "unicode subscript digits → ASCII"], "retrieval_domain": "physics", "dataset_name": "seephys", "question_id": "1577", "category": "EM", "has_image": true, "image_path": "/mnt/afs_reason/chendongyang/code/llm4math/ReForm/data/train/sft_collect/seeds/physics/seephys_images/1577.png"}
